# Q109 - Testing Cloud Run Revisions Before Production

## Scenario

A Cloud Run service is already deployed and available to customers.

The company wants developers to test the latest version of the application before it is exposed to production traffic.

The correct answer is:

**A. Run the `gcloud run deploy booking-engine --no-traffic --tag dev` command. Use the tagged URL for testing.**

---

# Why This Works

Cloud Run creates a new **revision** every time a new version of the application is deployed.

Normally, the latest revision receives production traffic immediately.

However, using the following options changes this behavior:

* `--no-traffic`
* `--tag dev`

The new revision is deployed successfully but receives **0% of the production traffic**.

At the same time, Cloud Run creates a dedicated URL for the tagged revision, allowing developers to test it independently.

For example:

```text
Production URL

https://booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app
```

```text
Development URL

https://dev--booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app
```

Customers continue using the production version while developers test the latest revision through the tagged URL.

---

# Why the Other Options Are Incorrect

## B. Route 1% of Traffic to the Latest Revision

Sending even 1% of production traffic means real users are testing the new version.

The requirement states that developers must test the revision **before it is exposed to customers**.

Therefore, this option does not satisfy the requirement.

## C. Identity Token

An identity token controls authentication.

It does not create a separate revision or prevent production traffic from reaching the latest deployment.

## D. Grant the Cloud Run Invoker Role

Granting the `roles/run.invoker` role allows developers to invoke the service.

However, it does not create a private testing revision or a separate testing URL.

---

# Relationship with Q108

This question is a continuation of the previous lab.

In Q108, a Cloud Run service was deployed using Terraform to monitor CPU and memory utilization with Cloud Monitoring.

The application architecture remained the same.

```text
                Internet
                    │
                    ▼
             Cloud Run Service
                    │
                    ▼
          Serverless Container
                    │
                    ▼
          Cloud Monitoring Metrics
```

In Q109, the infrastructure does not change.

Instead, a **new revision** is deployed without affecting production traffic.

```text
                Internet
                    │
                    ▼
             Cloud Run Service
                    │
        ┌───────────┴───────────┐
        │                       │
        ▼                       ▼
 Production Revision       Dev Revision
      100% Traffic            0% Traffic
           │                     │
           │                     │
           │          https://dev--service.run.app
           │                     │
      Customers             Developers
```

This approach allows safe testing without interrupting production.

---

# Lab Implementation

The lab starts from the Cloud Run service created in Q108.

A new revision is deployed using:

```bash
gcloud run deploy cost-optimization-demo \
    --image us-docker.pkg.dev/cloudrun/container/hello \
    --no-traffic \
    --tag dev \
    --region europe-west1
```

After deployment, the service configuration can be verified with:

```bash
gcloud run services describe cost-optimization-demo \
    --region europe-west1
```

Example output:

```text
Traffic:
  100% cost-optimization-demo-00001
  0%   cost-optimization-demo-00002
        dev: https://dev---cost-optimization-demo-xxxxx.run.app
```

The output confirms that:

* The production revision continues receiving 100% of the traffic.
* The new revision receives 0% of the traffic.
* Developers can access the new revision through the dedicated **dev** URL.

This deployment strategy enables safe testing before releasing a new version to production users.
